    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d9">

                <pb facs="0012" n="8" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg8"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/12027: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-10T10:00:00+08:00">January 10, 1938—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 10—9:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>17. Following for War Department from Colonel Stilwell.<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d9-f1"/></p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“I returned Hankow January 9 from Changsha, Nanchang, Kiukiang. No peace talk
                        anywhere in the area. Hunan Governor talks of 3 years’ resistance, Kiangsi
                        Governor same opinion. Training programs under way in Hunan and Kiangsi for
                        roughly 1 million men in each province. Munitions and equipment admitted
                        serious matter. Practically every Chinese soldier on guerilla warfare. Many
                        units withdrawn to Nanchang, Hankow area for replacement. I believe there is
                        little contact between opposing sides southwest of Nanking and that the
                        front there has practically melted away”.</p>
                </quote>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d9-f1" n="23" xml:id="d9-f1-content">
                <p>Col. Joseph W. Stilwell, Military Attaché in China.</p>
